// XFAIL: DF-212b a type declared in a CROSS-MODULE-EMBEDDED module gets a
// SECOND identity — `field `command` expects type `Cmd` but got `Cmd``, the
// same printed name on both sides, which is design 144's signature for one
// type resolved under two different (module, name) answers.
//
// A type keeps ONE identity however its defining module is reached. When
// module B's `main` transitively suspends, design 210's machinery embeds
// module A's suspending `Cli.parse` into B's frame; re-resolving A's own
// declarations while doing so must not mint a new `Cmd`. Note the error lands
// on a line that has nothing to do with the closure — the enum is simply
// declared in the module that got embedded.
//
// Load-bearing legs, each verified by bisection (removing any one silences it):
//   - `main` (the entry point) transitively suspends
//   - `parse` is made potentially-suspending by a non-`sync` function-typed
//     parameter that is passed a function value (marking it `sync` fixes it)
//   - `parse` is an EXTENSION METHOD (a free function hits a different error)
//   - `Cmd`/`Cli` are declared in the OTHER module (all-in-one-file compiles)
// The closure LITERAL is not required — a pre-bound closure value does it too.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// build
import std.task
import modules.embed_provider

func main() {
    let c = embed_provider.Cli.parse()
    print(embed_provider.name_of(&c))
    task.yield_now()
}
